Requirements: 9   with obligations: 9

[task-01] The shortest opening the client marks as reusable follows Anthropic's current
    documented minimum for each model: 512 tokens for Claude Sonnet 5.5, Opus 5.5 and
    Haiku 5.5. The module documentation says that Anthropic ignores a breakpoint below
    the minimum, rather than that it rejects one.
    -> min-reusable-prefix-per-model  [boundary/explicit, code_and_tests]
       The client's minimum prefix length for marking an opening as reusable (cacheable)
       is 512 tokens for each of Claude Sonnet 5.5, Opus 5.5 and Haiku 5.5, following
       Anthropic's documented minimum for each model.
    -> module-docs-state-anthropic-ignores-sub-minimum-breakpoint  [docs_config/explicit, code_only]
       The module documentation states that Anthropic ignores a cache breakpoint placed
       below the model's minimum token count, rather than stating that it rejects one.
       why code_only: The obligation concerns the wording of the module documentation,
       which a reader confirms by inspecting the docstring; no runtime behavior exists
       for a test to exercise.

[task-02] The defect-to-test pair mapping stage, which sends the most calls in a review,
    already places its defect list in the reusable opening, yet a recent review on
    Claude Sonnet 5.5 showed no cache reads on any stage. Repeated pair-mapping calls
    that share an opening are read from the cache.
    -> pair-mapping-defect-list-in-reusable-opening  [invariant/explicit, code_and_tests]
       Preserve the defect-to-test pair mapping stage's placement of its defect list in
       the reusable opening of each call, so that repeated pair-mapping calls share an
       identical opening that includes the defect list.
    -> sonnet-5-5-cache-reads-occur  [functional/inferred, code_and_tests]
       In a review run on Claude Sonnet 5.5, repeated calls that share a reusable
       opening are served from the prompt cache, so cache reads are reported for stages
       that send such calls, including the defect-to-test pair mapping stage, rather
       than zero cache reads on every stage.
    -> pair-mapping-repeat-calls-hit-cache  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       Repeated defect-to-test pair-mapping calls that share an opening are served from
       the prompt cache. The opening is built identically across those calls and marked
       as cacheable, so that calls after the first report cache reads on the model in
       use.

[task-03] The mutation descriptor stage sends one call per defect, and defects that
    share a region repeat that region's numbered source in every call. That source moves
    into the reusable opening so calls over the same region share it.
    -> mutation-descriptor-one-call-per-defect  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       The mutation descriptor stage sends exactly one call for each defect, so the
       number of calls equals the number of defects, including defects that share a
       region.
    -> region-source-in-shared-opening  [functional/inferred, code_and_tests]
       For defects that share a region, that region's numbered source is carried once in
       the reusable opening common to all calls over that region. The per-defect part of
       each call does not repeat it.
    -> region-source-in-reusable-opening  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       The numbered source of a region is placed in the reusable opening of the mutation
       descriptor calls, rather than in the per-defect portion of each call, so that
       calls over the same region share it.

[task-04] A run can opt in to sending its model calls through the Message Batches API.
    The setting belongs to the run's configuration and is off by default, so `acceptance
    check` and `acceptance decompose` keep calling the model directly. Benchmark scoring
    and experiment scripts can switch it on.
    -> batch-api-opt-in  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       A run can opt in to sending its model calls through the Message Batches API; when
       opted in, the run's model calls are submitted via the Message Batches API.
    -> batch-setting-in-run-config-default-off  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       The opt-in setting for sending model calls through the Message Batches API is
       part of the run's configuration and its default value is off.
    -> check-and-decompose-call-model-directly-by-default  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       With the batch setting at its default (off), `acceptance check` and `acceptance
       decompose` send their model calls directly to the model and not through the
       Message Batches API.
    -> batch-opt-in-switchable-by-scripts  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       Benchmark scoring and experiment scripts can enable the run-configuration setting
       that sends the run's model calls through the Message Batches API.

[task-05] How a call was sent does not change what was asked: a batched request is
    recorded under the same key as the same request sent directly, and its recording
    replays with no live call. The recording notes that it was sent through a batch. Two
    recorded runs over the same input still produce byte-identical review state
    whichever way their calls were sent.
    -> batched-request-same-recording-key  [invariant/explicit, code_and_tests]
       A batched request is recorded under the same key as the same request sent
       directly, so the recording key depends only on what was asked and not on whether
       the call was sent through a batch or directly.
    -> batched-recording-replays-offline  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       A recording made from a batched request replays without making any live call.
    -> recording-notes-batch-origin  [functional/explicit, code_and_tests]
       A recording of a request that was sent through a batch carries a note indicating
       it was sent through a batch.
    -> identical-review-state-across-send-modes  [invariant/explicit, code_and_tests]
       Two recorded runs over the same input produce byte-identical review state,
       whether their calls were sent directly or through a batch.

